## Abstract

This dissertation presents the first complete theoretical framework for Electromagnetically Assisted Terminal Landing Infrastructure (EATLI): an architecture in which the terminal phase of reusable rocket descent is actively shared between the vehicle and a permanently instrumented landing site equipped with electromagnetic field arrays, magnetorheological damping structures, and regenerative power electronics. The work makes eight formal contributions across four theoretical domains.

In the **electromagnetic domain**, we derive the full non-linear braking force profile accounting for the Magnetic Reynolds Number transition from the linear eddy-current regime to the high-velocity saturation regime, identifying the bimodal load structure that emerges as a vehicle decelerates through the critical velocity $v_{\text{crit}}$. In the **optimisation domain**, we present a complete Second-Order Cone Programme (SOCP) formulation of the EATLI energy partition problem grounded in lossless convexification, and prove via the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation that infrastructure dissipation forces monotonically reduce the propellant-optimal thrust requirement. In the **control domain**, we construct the Terminal Capture Funnel using a Control Lyapunov Function (CLF) and prove its strict expansion under infrastructure assistance, then introduce a unified Control Barrier Function–CLF Quadratic Programme (CBF-CLF-QP) that enforces safety-critical phase transitions in real time. In the **economic-civilisational domain**, we derive the Infrastructure-Vehicle Mass Transfer Inequality from first principles via the two-phase Tsiolkovsky equation, quantify the EATLI-ISRU feedback loop on Mars and the Moon through the Mars Surface Mass Efficiency construct, and demonstrate that EATLI constitutes a structural multiplier for the economic viability of planetary colonisation.

Four architectural proposals — the Smart Skin Co-Design Framework, the CBF safety switching layer, the Closed-Loop ISRU Value-Nexus, and the Stochastic Aerodynamic Wake Model — are developed in full and integrated into the mathematical core.

**Keywords**: terminal descent, electromagnetic braking, magnetic Reynolds number, MR dampers, SOCP, lossless convexification, Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman, Control Lyapunov Function, Control Barrier Function, reusable launch vehicles, ISRU, planetary colonisation

