## Part I — The Structural Problem

### Chapter 1 — The Terminal Landing Mass Paradox

Every gram of hardware dedicated exclusively to landing is, by the Tsiolkovsky equation, a gram of payload sacrificed on every flight for the entire operational life of the vehicle. This asymmetry — that landing hardware pays the same mass tax as payload but delivers no mission value — is the Terminal Landing Mass Paradox: the systems required to make a rocket reusable partially defeat the economic logic of reusability.

Modern commercial reusable systems explicitly accept a performance penalty by reserving a significant portion of propellant for landing manoeuvres in exchange for the economic benefits of reusability. [Circularastronomy](https://circularastronomy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/rocket-reusability-literature-review.pdf) SpaceX abandoned plans to recover the Falcon 9 second stage precisely because the additional mass of the required heat shield, landing gear, and low-powered landing engines would incur too great a performance penalty. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SpaceX_reusable_launch_system_development_program) This decision illustrates the boundary condition of the vehicle-centric paradigm: for sufficiently large vehicles or demanding mission profiles, the mass tax of onboard landing capability exceeds the economic benefit of recovery.

More than 90% of a rocket's liftoff mass is typically propellant and tanks, while less than 4% is actual payload. [Shankar IAS Parliament](https://www.shankariasparliament.com/current-affairs/reusable-rocket-technology) Within this razor-thin mass budget, every kilogram of landing system displaces payload exponentially through the Tsiolkovsky equation. The paradox cannot be resolved by optimising within the vehicle-centric paradigm. It requires a paradigm shift.

The EATLI framework provides this shift. Its foundational reallocation is simple: mass removed from the vehicle and invested in permanent ground infrastructure does not travel to orbit. Infrastructure mass is amortised across potentially thousands of landings, paying a fractional mass tax per flight that shrinks with launch cadence. At sufficient cadence, the mass-economic advantage of infrastructure over vehicle-borne capability is not marginal — it is transformative.

SpaceX Starship's first mid-air catch of the Super Heavy booster in October 2024 using Mechazilla tower arms eliminated the need for landing legs and their associated mass penalty [Orbit Codex](https://orbitcodex.com/knowledge-base/reusable-rockets) , directly validating this principle at scale. EATLI proposes the electromagnetic and thermodynamic completion of what Mechazilla began mechanically.

