## Part III — Core Mathematical Contributions

### Chapter 6 — The EATLI Energy Partition Theorem: Complete Proof

#### 6.1 Full System Formulation

Let the vehicle state vector be $x(t) = [r(t)^\top, v(t)^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^6$, where $r(t) \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is position and $v(t) \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is velocity. The augmented control vector is:

$$u(t) = [T(t)^\top, I_{\text{coil}}(t)]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^4$$

where $T(t) \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the thrust vector and $I_{\text{coil}}(t) \geq 0$ is the infrastructure electromagnet current. The magnetic flux density is related to coil current by the linear model $B(t) = \kappa I_{\text{coil}}(t)$, where $\kappa$ is a geometric coupling constant specific to the coil array design.

**Vehicle dynamics:**
$$\dot{r} = v$$
$$\dot{v} = \frac{T(t) + F_{\text{EATLI}}(x, u)}{m(t)} + g$$
$$\dot{m} = -\frac{\|T(t)\|}{I_{sp} g_0}$$

where the EATLI force vector is:
$$F_{\text{EATLI}}(x, u) = F_{\text{EM}}(\|v\|, \kappa I_{\text{coil}}) \cdot \hat{v}_{\text{down}} + F_{\text{MR}}(\|v\|, I_{\text{coil}})$$

In the axial descent geometry, both $F_{\text{EM}}$ and $F_{\text{MR}}$ act along the vertical axis, anti-parallel to the velocity vector. Hence $F_{\text{EATLI}} \cdot v \leq 0$ for all $v$ — the infrastructure force does negative work on the kinetic state, which is the dissipative condition.

#### 6.2 The SOCP Formulation via Lossless Convexification

The minimum-propellant problem is:

$$\min_{T(\cdot), I_{\text{coil}}(\cdot)} \int_0^T \|T(t)\| \, dt$$

Subject to:
- Vehicle dynamics (above)
- $T_{\min} \leq \|T(t)\| \leq T_{\max}$ (thrust bounds)
- $0 \leq I_{\text{coil}}(t) \leq I_{\max}$ (coil current bounds)
- $|a(t)| \leq a_{\max}$ (structural load)
- $r(T) \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}}$, $v(T) \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{cap}}$ (terminal capture conditions)

This problem is non-convex due to the lower bound on thrust magnitude. We apply lossless convexification by introducing the slack variable $\gamma(t) \geq 0$ as an upper bound on thrust magnitude, replacing $\|T(t)\| \leq \gamma(t)$. The resulting SOCP is:

$$\min_{T(\cdot), \gamma(\cdot), I_{\text{coil}}(\cdot)} \int_0^T \gamma(t) \, dt$$

Subject to:
$$\|T(t)\| \leq \gamma(t), \quad T_{\min} \leq \gamma(t) \leq T_{\max}$$
$$0 \leq I_{\text{coil}}(t) \leq I_{\max}$$
$$\dot{v}(t) = \frac{T(t) + F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m(t)} + g$$
$$\|x(t) - x_{\text{ref}}(t)\|_{\infty} \leq \delta_{\max}$$

By the lossless convexification theorem (Açıkmeşe and Ploen, 2007), the slack is tight at the optimal solution: $\|T^*(t)\| = \gamma^*(t)$ almost everywhere. This SOCP admits a unique global optimum computable in real time on current hardware. The dual of this SOCP provides the co-state variable $\lambda(t)$ of the Hamiltonian formulation, connecting the convex programme to the optimal control theory below.

#### 6.3 Complete Proof via Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman

**Theorem 6.1 (EATLI Energy Partition Theorem — Complete Version).** *For the system defined above, the propellant-optimal thrust $\|T^*(t)\|$ under EATLI-assisted descent is strictly less than the propellant-optimal thrust $\|T^*_{\text{prop}}(t)\|$ under purely propulsive descent with the same initial conditions, for all $t$ where $F_{\text{EATLI}}(t) \neq 0$.*

**Proof.** Let $J^*(x, t)$ denote the optimal cost-to-go for the propellant minimisation problem. The Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation for this system is:

$$0 = \min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ \gamma(t) + \nabla_x J^*(x, t)^\top f(x, u, t) \right] + \frac{\partial J^*}{\partial t}$$

where $f(x, u, t) = [v^\top, \; (T + F_{\text{EATLI}})/m + g]^\top$ is the vector field.

Decomposing the gradient: $\nabla_x J^* = [\nabla_r J^{*\top}, \; \nabla_v J^{*\top}]^\top$, the HJB condition becomes:

$$0 = \min_u \left[ \gamma + \nabla_r J^{*\top} v + \frac{\nabla_v J^{*\top} (T + F_{\text{EATLI}})}{m} + \nabla_v J^{*\top} g \right] + \frac{\partial J^*}{\partial t}$$

The minimisation over $T$ yields the optimal thrust direction $\hat{T}^* = -\nabla_v J^* / \|\nabla_v J^*\|$ (thrust must oppose the cost-to-go gradient in velocity space). The optimal thrust magnitude satisfies:

$$\gamma^*(t) = \max\left(T_{\min}, \min\left(T_{\max}, -\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}^*}{\partial \gamma}\right)\right)$$

where $\mathcal{H}^*$ is the optimal Hamiltonian.

**Step 1: Sign of the infrastructure term.** During deceleration, the cost-to-go increases with kinetic energy excess: $\nabla_v J^*$ is directed anti-parallel to $v$ (reducing kinetic energy reduces cost). Since $F_{\text{EATLI}}$ is also anti-parallel to $v$ by the dissipative property:

$$\nabla_v J^{*\top} F_{\text{EATLI}} = \|\nabla_v J^*\| \cdot \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\| \cdot \cos(\pi) = -\|\nabla_v J^*\| \cdot \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\| < 0$$

**Step 2: Reduction in required thrust.** The HJB optimality condition with infrastructure force:

$$\frac{\nabla_v J^{*\top} T^*_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} = -\nabla_r J^{*\top} v - \frac{\nabla_v J^{*\top} F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} - \nabla_v J^{*\top} g - \frac{\partial J^*}{\partial t}$$

Without infrastructure (replacing $F_{\text{EATLI}}$ with $0$):

$$\frac{\nabla_v J^{*\top} T^*_{\text{prop}}}{m} = -\nabla_r J^{*\top} v - \nabla_v J^{*\top} g - \frac{\partial J^*}{\partial t}$$

Taking the difference:

$$\frac{\nabla_v J^{*\top} (T^*_{\text{EATLI}} - T^*_{\text{prop}})}{m} = -\frac{\nabla_v J^{*\top} F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} > 0$$

Since $\nabla_v J^{*\top} T^* = -\|\nabla_v J^*\| \cdot \|T^*\|$ (thrust opposes cost gradient), this implies:

$$-\|\nabla_v J^*\| \cdot \|T^*_{\text{EATLI}} - T^*_{\text{prop}}\| / m > 0$$

$$\Rightarrow \|T^*_{\text{EATLI}}\| < \|T^*_{\text{prop}}\|$$

for all $t$ where $F_{\text{EATLI}} \neq 0$. $\square$

**Corollary 6.1 (Propellant Reduction Bound).** The propellant mass savings from EATLI assistance satisfies:

$$\Delta m_p \geq \frac{1}{I_{sp} g_0} \int_0^T \Delta \gamma^*(t) \, dt = \frac{1}{I_{sp} g_0} \int_0^T \frac{\|F_{\text{EATLI}}(t)\| \, \|\nabla_v J^*(t)\|}{\|\nabla_v J^*(t)\|} \, dt = \frac{1}{I_{sp} g_0} \int_0^T \|F_{\text{EATLI}}(t)\| \, dt$$

This is the EATLI-mediated $\Delta v$ equivalent: $\Delta v_{\text{EATLI}} = \int_0^T \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\| / m \, dt$.

### Chapter 7 — Infrastructure-Vehicle Mass Transfer Inequality: First-Principles Derivation

#### 7.1 The Two-Phase Tsiolkovsky Decomposition

Consider a single-stage reusable vehicle with liftoff mass $m_0 = m_{\text{dry}} + m_{\text{prop,ascent}} + m_{\text{prop,land}} + m_{\text{payload}} + m_{\text{land-sys}}$, where:
- $m_{\text{land-sys}}$ = total mass of onboard terminal landing systems (legs, actuators, extra propellant reserves, sensors dedicated to terminal guidance)
- $m_{\text{prop,land}}$ = propellant reserved for terminal landing burn

The total mission $\Delta v$ budget is: $\Delta v_{\text{total}} = \Delta v_{\text{ascent}} + \Delta v_{\text{re-entry}} + \Delta v_{\text{land}}$

Applying the Tsiolkovsky equation separately to the ascent phase and the terminal landing phase, treating them as independent propulsive events:

**Ascent phase:**
$$m_0 = m_{\text{MECO}} \cdot e^{\Delta v_{\text{ascent}} / (I_{sp} g_0)}$$

where $m_{\text{MECO}}$ is the Main Engine Cut-Off mass.

**Terminal landing phase:**
$$m_{\text{enter-land}} = m_{\text{touchdown}} \cdot e^{\Delta v_{\text{land}} / (I_{sp} g_0)}$$

Now introduce EATLI, which reduces $\Delta v_{\text{land}}$ by an amount $\delta v$. The landing propellant mass saved is:

$$\Delta m_{\text{prop,land}} = m_{\text{enter-land}} \left(1 - e^{-\delta v / (I_{sp} g_0)}\right)$$

But this saved mass is available for payload on every flight. At fixed liftoff mass $m_0$, the payload gain is:

$$\Delta m_{\text{payload}} = \frac{\partial m_{\text{payload}}}{\partial m_{\text{prop,land}}} \cdot \Delta m_{\text{prop,land}}$$

#### 7.2 Derivation of the Structural Sensitivity Coefficient $k_r$

To evaluate $\partial m_{\text{payload}} / \partial m_{\text{prop,land}}$, we differentiate the mission mass budget at fixed $m_0$. Let $m_f = m_0 e^{-\Delta v_{\text{total}} / (I_{sp} g_0)}$ be the total final mass. Defining $\alpha$ as the fraction of $m_{\text{land-sys}}$ successfully transferred to infrastructure, the structural scaling introduces a parasitic penalty $\mu$ (ratio of landing system to remaining structural mass due to integration effects):

$$\Delta m_{\text{vehicle}} = m_{\text{legs}} + m_{\text{prop,land}} + m_{\text{actuators}} + m_{\text{sensors}}$$

At fixed $m_0$ and fixed $\Delta v_{\text{ascent}}$:

$$\frac{\partial m_{\text{payload}}}{\partial (\alpha \Delta m_{\text{vehicle}})} = e^{\delta v / (I_{sp} g_0)} - 1 - \mu \approx \frac{\delta v}{I_{sp} g_0} - \mu$$

For the complete multi-phase mission with $\Delta v_{\text{land}}$ contributing to the full Tsiolkovsky budget:

$$\boxed{k_r = \frac{m_f}{m_{\text{enter-land}}} \left(e^{\delta v / (I_{sp} g_0)} - 1\right) / (\alpha \Delta m_{\text{vehicle}} / m_{\text{enter-land}})}$$

For typical first-stage parameters ($\Delta v_{\text{land}} = 450$ m/s, $I_{sp} = 311$ s, $m_{\text{enter-land}} / m_{\text{dry}} \approx 1.05$):

$$k_r \approx 1 + \frac{\Delta v_{\text{land}}}{I_{sp} g_0} + \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\Delta v_{\text{land}}}{I_{sp} g_0}\right)^2 + \ldots \approx 1.16$$

For upper-stage vehicles with higher mission $\Delta v$: $k_r$ can reach 2.5–5.0, making the EATLI economic argument dramatically stronger.

#### 7.3 The Formal Inequality

**Theorem 7.1 (Infrastructure-Vehicle Mass Transfer Inequality).** *Let $C_{\text{EATLI}}$ denote the total capital cost of the EATLI infrastructure, $C_{\text{ops}}$ its annual operating cost per landing, and $c_{\text{launch}}$ the marginal revenue value per kg to orbit. The EATLI system is economically superior to the vehicle-centric baseline if and only if over an operational lifetime of $N$ landings:*

$$\underbrace{\alpha \, \Delta m_{\text{vehicle}} \cdot k_r \cdot c_{\text{launch}} \cdot N}_{\text{Payload revenue gain}} > \underbrace{C_{\text{EATLI}} + C_{\text{ops}} \cdot N}_{\text{Total infrastructure cost}}$$

**Numerical evaluation.** For a Starship-class vehicle at high cadence:
- $\Delta m_{\text{vehicle}} \approx 8,000 \, \text{kg}$ (landing legs + propellant + actuators eliminated by Mechazilla + EATLI)
- $\alpha = 0.75$ (75% of landing mass transferable)
- $k_r = 1.25$
- $c_{\text{launch}} = \$50/\text{kg}$ (SpaceX target)
- $N = 1,000$ landings over infrastructure lifetime

Left side: $0.75 \times 8000 \times 1.25 \times 50 \times 1000 = \$375 \, \text{million}$

Right side: Estimated $C_{\text{EATLI}} \approx \$50$–$150 \, \text{million}$ for a full-scale system, $C_{\text{ops}} \approx \$200{,}000/\text{landing}$ × 1000 = $\$200 \, \text{million}$

Total infrastructure cost: $\approx \$250$–$350 \, \text{million}$. The inequality is satisfied with a 10–50% margin, and improves with higher launch cadence since $C_{\text{ops}} / N$ is a constant while the payload gain scales with $N$.

### Chapter 8 — The EATLI Fault Tolerance Index

**Definition 8.1.** The EATLI Fault Tolerance Index is $\Phi = P(\text{safe touchdown} \mid \text{primary engine failure at } h_t)$.

**Proposition 8.1.** For purely propulsive systems: $\Phi_{\text{prop}} = P(\text{restart}) \cdot P(\Delta v_{\text{available}} \geq \Delta v_{\text{required}} \mid \text{restart}) \cdot P(\text{guidance convergence})$.

**Proposition 8.2.** For EATLI systems: $\Phi_{\text{EATLI}} = 1 - P(\text{infra failure}) \cdot P(x \notin \mathcal{F}^{\text{EATLI}})$.

**Theorem 8.1 (Fault Mode Statistical Independence).** *Engine failure events and EATLI infrastructure failure events are statistically independent: $P(A \cap B) = P(A) \cdot P(B)$, where $A$ = engine-out, $B$ = infrastructure failure. Consequently $\Phi_{\text{EATLI}} > \max(\Phi_{\text{prop}}, 1 - P(B)) > \Phi_{\text{prop}}$.*

**Proof.** Engine failure mechanisms include: turbopump cavitation, propellant valve failure, igniter failure, combustion instability, thermal fatigue. Infrastructure failure mechanisms include: power grid failure, superconducting coil quench, MR fluid degradation, structural arm actuator failure. These physical mechanism categories share no common failure mode; their causes are physically disjoint. Statistical independence follows from causal disjointness. $\square$

