## Part IV — Control Theory

### Chapter 9 — The Terminal Capture Funnel: CLF Formulation and Proof

#### 9.1 The Control Lyapunov Function

Define the tracking CLF as the weighted quadratic form:

$$V(x) = (r - r_{\text{cap}})^\top Q (r - r_{\text{cap}}) + (v - v_{\text{ref}}(t))^\top P (v - v_{\text{ref}}(t))$$

where $r_{\text{cap}}$ is the centre of the electromagnetic capture zone, $v_{\text{ref}}(t)$ is the reference velocity profile (from G-FOLD or pre-computed offline), and $Q, P \succ 0$ are positive definite weighting matrices tuned to the landing geometry.

The CLF condition for exponential convergence at rate $\lambda > 0$:

$$\dot{V}(x, u) \leq -\lambda V(x)$$

Expanding the time derivative:

$$\dot{V} = 2(r - r_{\text{cap}})^\top Q \dot{r} + 2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P (\dot{v} - \dot{v}_{\text{ref}})$$

$$= 2(r - r_{\text{cap}})^\top Q v + 2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P \left(\frac{T + F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} + g - \dot{v}_{\text{ref}}\right)$$

#### 9.2 The Terminal Capture Funnel

**Definition 9.1.** The Terminal Capture Funnel $\mathcal{F}(\tau)$ at time $\tau$ before touchdown is:

$$\mathcal{F}(\tau) = \left\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{X} : \exists u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{feasible}}, \; \dot{V}(x(t), u(t)) \leq -\lambda V(x(t)) \; \forall t \in [0, \tau] \right\}$$

The boundary $\partial \mathcal{F}(\tau)$ is characterised by the states where the CLF condition holds with equality under the maximum available control.

#### 9.3 Proof of Strict Funnel Expansion under EATLI

**Theorem 9.1 (Funnel Expansion Theorem).** $\mathcal{F}^{\text{prop}}(\tau) \subsetneq \mathcal{F}^{\text{EATLI}}(\tau)$ for all $\tau \geq 0$ whenever $F_{\text{EATLI}} \neq 0$.

**Proof.** The CLF condition requires finding $T \in \mathcal{U}_T$ such that:

$$2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P \frac{T}{m} \leq -\lambda V - 2(r-r_{\text{cap}})^\top Q v - 2(v-v_{\text{ref}})^\top P \left(\frac{F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} + g - \dot{v}_{\text{ref}}\right)$$

Define the residual driving term as:

$$\Delta(x, u) = -\lambda V - 2(r-r_{\text{cap}})^\top Q v - 2(v-v_{\text{ref}})^\top P (g - \dot{v}_{\text{ref}})$$

**Without EATLI**: The condition is $2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P T / m \leq \Delta(x)$.

**With EATLI**: The condition is $2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P T / m \leq \Delta(x) + \psi_{\text{EATLI}}(x, u)$, where:

$$\psi_{\text{EATLI}}(x, u) = -\frac{2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P \, F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m}$$

Since $F_{\text{EATLI}}$ acts anti-parallel to $v$ and $P \succ 0$, and for $v \neq v_{\text{ref}}$ (which holds throughout the approach):

$$\psi_{\text{EATLI}} = \frac{2(v - v_{\text{ref}})^\top P \, |F_{\text{EATLI}}|}{m} \cdot \text{sgn}(-(v-v_{\text{ref}}) \cdot F_{\text{EATLI}}) > 0$$

The right-hand side of the CLF condition strictly increases by $\psi_{\text{EATLI}} > 0$. This strictly relaxes the constraint on $T$, meaning every state that was marginally outside $\mathcal{F}^{\text{prop}}(\tau)$ (where no feasible $T$ existed) now has a feasible $T$ in $\mathcal{F}^{\text{EATLI}}(\tau)$. Therefore $\mathcal{F}^{\text{prop}}(\tau) \subsetneq \mathcal{F}^{\text{EATLI}}(\tau)$. $\square$

**Corollary 9.1 (Quantified Expansion).** The linear expansion of the capture funnel boundary along the velocity axis is:

$$\|v_{\text{max}}^{\text{EATLI}} - v_{\text{max}}^{\text{prop}}\| \geq \frac{\psi_{\text{EATLI}} \cdot m}{2 \lambda_{\min}(P) \cdot T_{\text{range}}}$$

This corollary provides a computable lower bound on funnel expansion, enabling infrastructure sizing from capture probability requirements.

