## Part V — Advanced Architectural Contributions

### Chapter 10 — Proposal 1: The Smart Skin Co-Design Framework

The v0.2 formulation treated the vehicle as a passive cylindrical conductor. This chapter introduces the **Smart Skin**: an active electromagnetic interface layer co-designed between the vehicle structural ring and the infrastructure field system, transforming the EATLI interaction from a drag-passive mechanism into a **split-stator linear synchronous motor architecture**.

#### 10.1 Material Architecture

The Smart Skin is a three-layer composite structure integrated into the vehicle's base structural ring:

**Layer 1 — Outer Eddy-Current Generation Shell**: A copper-aluminium matrix alloy (Cu-Al, $\sigma_{\text{eff}} \approx 3.5 \times 10^7 \, \text{S/m}$, density $\approx 3.0 \, \text{g/cm}^3$) of thickness $3$–$5 \, \text{mm}$. This layer is the primary eddy-current generator, providing 25× the electromagnetic coupling force per unit field of standard stainless steel. The reduced $v_{\text{crit}} \approx 4.5$ m/s means the bimodal braking profile covers the entire terminal descent velocity range, maximising the integral of deceleration force over the approach.

**Layer 2 — Structural Load-Bearing Core**: Carbon-fibre reinforced polymer (CFRP) composite, providing structural integrity and thermal insulation between Layer 1 and Layer 3. This layer carries the mechanical loads of capture and maintains geometric stability of the electromagnetic interface under thermal gradients.

**Layer 3 — Inner High-Remanence Superconducting Film**: REBCO (Rare Earth Barium Copper Oxide) coated-conductor tape arrays, operated at or above their transition temperature $T_c \approx 93 \, \text{K}$ using cryogenic cooling channels. This layer enables **flux pinning**: when the infrastructure field is applied, the superconducting film traps the field geometry, providing passive attitude stabilisation. A vehicle entering the EATLI field with slight angular deviation is automatically corrected toward field alignment by the flux-pinning restoring torque.

**Mass budget**: The complete Smart Skin assembly for a Starship-class base ring (~12 m diameter) is estimated at 800–1,500 kg — comparable to one set of landing legs. This mass is onboard, but unlike landing legs, it serves a structural function throughout flight (as thermal protection and structural reinforcement), amortising its mass tax.

#### 10.2 The Split-Stator Linear Synchronous Motor Analogy

In a conventional linear synchronous motor:
- The **stator** generates a travelling magnetic field wave
- The **rotor** (translator) carries permanent magnets that synchronise with the wave

In the EATLI Smart Skin architecture:
- The **infrastructure coil array** plays the role of stator, generating a controlled field distribution
- The **vehicle Smart Skin** plays the role of translator, carrying the conductive and superconducting layers

The deceleration phase corresponds to regenerative braking in a linear motor: the translator (vehicle) decelerates relative to the stator (infrastructure), and the relative motion generates back-EMF that is harvested by the power electronics. The recovery efficiency $\eta_{\text{rec}}$ is determined by the coupling quality between the two layers — a design parameter optimised jointly across vehicle and infrastructure.

**Key upgrade over passive eddy-current braking**: The split-stator architecture allows the infrastructure to **actively shape the braking force profile** by controlling the spatial and temporal distribution of the field wave. This converts the braking force from a passive, velocity-proportional drag into an actively programmable deceleration trajectory — directly interfacing with the G-FOLD guidance layer to execute precisely the optimal partition $\mathbf{E}^*$ from Theorem 6.1.

#### 10.3 Force Enhancement Factor

The electromagnetic braking force with Smart Skin versus standard steel:

$$\frac{F_{\text{EM}}^{\text{smart}}}{F_{\text{EM}}^{\text{steel}}} = \frac{\sigma_{\text{Cu-Al}} \cdot A_{\text{eff,smart}}}{\sigma_{\text{steel}} \cdot A_{\text{eff,steel}}} \approx \frac{3.5 \times 10^7 \times 1.4}{1.4 \times 10^6 \times 1.0} = 35$$

At equal applied field strength, the Smart Skin delivers 35× the eddy-current braking force, reducing the required magnetic flux density $B_{\text{req}}$ by a factor of $\sqrt{35} \approx 5.9$ for the same target force. This brings required field strengths into the range of conventional water-cooled electromagnets (0.5–2 T) rather than requiring superconducting coil arrays, dramatically reducing infrastructure cost and complexity.

### Chapter 11 — Proposal 2: Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Phase Transitions

#### 11.1 The Safety-Critical Transition Problem

The three-phase EATLI descent (Phase α: EM pre-deceleration, Phase β: mechanical capture, Phase γ: post-capture stabilisation) involves two critical transitions where incorrect timing constitutes a catastrophic failure mode:

**Transition 1 (α → β)**: If the vehicle enters the mechanical capture zone at excessive velocity, the MR dampers are overwhelmed and the vehicle impacts the capture arms with destructive force. If the vehicle enters too slowly or with excessive angular deviation, the capture arms fail to engage.

**Transition 2 (β → γ)**: If the capture is not mechanically secured before engine shutdown, the vehicle may slip free. If engines are not shut down on capture confirmation, uncontrolled thrust after contact causes structural failure.

Standard CLF-based controllers guarantee convergence but provide no hard safety guarantees for these discrete transition events. Control Barrier Functions, introduced by Ames et al., provide a rigorous method for enforcing forward invariance of safe sets in safety-critical systems, unifiable with CLFs in a QP framework that allows the achievement of control objectives subject to safety constraints. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.06408)

#### 11.2 Formal CBF Definitions

**Definition 11.1 (Control Barrier Function).** A continuously differentiable function $h: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ is a Control Barrier Function for the EATLI system if the safe set $\mathcal{S} = \{x \in \mathcal{X} : h(x) \geq 0\}$ satisfies:

$$\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ \dot{h}(x, u) + \alpha(h(x)) \right] \geq 0 \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{S}$$

where $\alpha: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is a class $\mathcal{K}$ function. This condition ensures that the system can always be driven away from the boundary of the safe set, guaranteeing forward invariance.

#### 11.3 The Three EATLI Safety Barriers

**Barrier 1 — Tower Collision Prevention (Phase α):**

$$h_1(x) = \|r(t) - r_{\text{tower}}\|^2 - r_{\text{min}}^2$$

This enforces a minimum stand-off distance between the vehicle centre line and the capture tower structure during the electromagnetic pre-deceleration phase. $\dot{h}_1(x,u) = 2(r - r_{\text{tower}})^\top v$; the CBF condition requires that the vehicle can always be steered away from the tower if the approach angle becomes dangerous.

**Barrier 2 — Mechanical Capture Velocity Bound (Phase α → β):**

$$h_2(x) = v_{\text{cap,max}}^2 - \|v(t)\|^2$$

This ensures the vehicle velocity is below the MR damper absorption limit $v_{\text{cap,max}}$ before entering the capture zone. $\dot{h}_2(x,u) = -2v^\top \dot{v} = -2v^\top (T + F_{\text{EATLI}})/m - 2v^\top g$. The CBF condition requires: if velocity approaches $v_{\text{cap,max}}$, both the propulsive and EATLI forces must be sufficient to decelerate further.

**Barrier 3 — Engine-Out Propellant Reserve (all phases):**

$$h_3(x) = m_p(t) - m_{\text{abort,min}}$$

This enforces a minimum propellant reserve $m_{\text{abort,min}}$ sufficient to execute an abort trajectory (divert away from the tower) at any point during the EATLI approach. Through the CBF construction, safety conditions specified as forward invariance of a set are verified via Lyapunov-like conditions, and each formulation yields inequality constraints in the control input that, when satisfied, imply forward invariance. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.06408)

#### 11.4 The Unified CBF-CLF Quadratic Programme

The unified EATLI controller solves the following QP at every time step (targeting real-time execution at 1 kHz):

$$\min_{u, \delta} \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|_H^2 + p \delta^2$$

Subject to:
$$\underbrace{\dot{V}(x, u) + \lambda V(x) \leq \delta}_{\text{CLF (soft — allows slack})} \tag{stability}$$
$$\underbrace{\dot{h}_i(x, u) + \alpha_i(h_i(x)) \geq 0, \quad i = 1, 2, 3}_{\text{CBF (hard constraints)}} \tag{safety}$$
$$u \in \mathcal{U} \tag{actuator limits}$$

where $u_{\text{nom}}$ is the nominal G-FOLD trajectory tracking command, $\delta \geq 0$ is a slack variable allowing CLF relaxation when safety constraints conflict with convergence, and $p \gg 1$ penalises slack heavily to keep convergence tight. Safety (CBF) constraints are **hard** — they are never relaxed. Convergence (CLF) is **soft** — it can be temporarily sacrificed to maintain safety. The QP generates real-time control actions that prioritise safety if the objectives conflict and relax the stabilisation requirement only as necessary. [Emergent Mind](https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/control-barrier-functions-cbfs)

This QP is convex, has a unique solution for any fixed $x$ (since $H \succ 0$), and can be solved analytically via its KKT conditions without requiring iterative methods — enabling verified execution within 100 μs on flight-grade processors.

