## Part VI — The Extraterrestrial Case

### Chapter 12 — Comparative Planetary Analysis

Before developing the ISRU value-nexus, we establish the planetary hierarchy of EATLI value by examining the energy budget in each environment.

**Earth (atmospheric)**: Terminal descent from 2 km at 50 m/s. Aerodynamic drag provides approximately 30–50% of required deceleration energy in Phase B. EATLI supplements the remaining propulsive requirement. The case for EATLI is economic (mass savings, fault tolerance) but not existential.

**Moon (vacuum, $g_{\text{Moon}} = 1.62 \, \text{m/s}^2$)**: No atmosphere. Every joule of kinetic energy from orbital descent must be propulsively dissipated. Terminal velocity from a low lunar orbit insertion is approximately 1,700 m/s. The complete terminal deceleration burn (from, say, 100 m/s to rest) requires a $\Delta v_{\text{land}} \approx 100$ m/s and propellant mass fraction $m_p / m_{\text{land}} \approx 1 - e^{-100/(311 \times 1.62)} \approx 17$% — for a 10-tonne lander, approximately 1.7 tonnes of propellant for the final 100 m alone. EATLI can absorb a significant portion of this. The case is strongly economic.

**Mars (thin atmosphere, $g_{\text{Mars}} = 3.72 \, \text{m/s}^2$)**: Atmospheric surface pressure approximately 0.6% of Earth's. Aerodynamic deceleration from supersonic entry to subsonic terminal is feasible but provides a terminal velocity still in the range of 50–200 m/s for heavy vehicles — requiring substantial propulsive landing. According to ISRU mass budget analyses, every 1 kg of propellant produced on the Moon or Mars saves 7.4 to 11.3 kg in LEO [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.08567) , making any reduction in surface propellant consumption a massive LEO mass multiplier.

### Chapter 13 — Proposal 3: The Closed-Loop ISRU Value-Nexus

#### 13.1 The Sabatier-Electrolysis Propellant Chain

On Mars, propellant production proceeds via two reactions:

**Sabatier reaction** (methane synthesis):
$$\text{CO}_2 + 4\text{H}_2 \xrightarrow{\text{Ru, 250°C}} \text{CH}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}$$

**Water electrolysis** (oxygen and hydrogen production):
$$2\text{H}_2\text{O} \xrightarrow{\text{electrolysis}} 2\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2$$

A membrane Sabatier system integrating a catalytic reactor with an H₂O vapor permselective membrane achieves substantial CO₂ conversion, high CH₄ yield, and excellent long-term stability, with robustness under intermittent power supplies making it highly compatible with Martian solar energy conditions. [Nature](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-63667-w)

NASA analysis shows that a full ISRU system for producing both liquid methane and liquid oxygen from Mars resources would weigh approximately 1.7 tonnes while eliminating the need to transport 30 tonnes of ascent propellants from Earth. [NASA Technical Reports Server](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20170001421) The leverage ratio is approximately 17.6:1 — for every kilogram of ISRU plant mass pre-deployed to Mars, 17.6 kg of Earth-launched propellant mass is avoided.

#### 13.2 The EATLI-ISRU Feedback Loop

Define the Mars Surface Mass Efficiency (MSME) as:

$$\epsilon_{\text{MS}} = \frac{m_{\text{useful,surface}}}{m_{\text{total,transported from Earth}}}$$

For a Mars landing without EATLI, the propellant mass fraction devoted to terminal landing reduces $\epsilon_{\text{MS}}$ by a factor $\phi_{\text{land}}$:

$$\epsilon_{\text{MS}}^{\text{baseline}} = \epsilon_{\text{MS}}^{\text{max}} \cdot (1 - \phi_{\text{land}})$$

EATLI reduces $\phi_{\text{land}}$ by fraction $\rho_{\text{EATLI}}$:

$$\epsilon_{\text{MS}}^{\text{EATLI}} = \epsilon_{\text{MS}}^{\text{max}} \cdot (1 - (1 - \rho_{\text{EATLI}}) \phi_{\text{land}})$$

But the feedback goes deeper. The ISRU plant size needed to fuel Mars operations scales with the total propellant consumption rate, including landing. A 30% reduction in landing propellant requirement reduces ISRU plant size by approximately 30%, which:

**First-order effect**: Reduces the mass that must be pre-transported from Earth to build the ISRU plant: $\Delta M_{\text{ISRU}} = -0.30 \times \rho_{\text{ISRU}} \dot{m}_{\text{prop}}$

**Second-order effect**: Smaller ISRU plant → less power system to operate it → lighter power infrastructure → less mass to land → less landing propellant → still smaller ISRU plant needed.

This creates a **compounding efficiency loop**. Modelling this as a geometric series:

$$\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \Delta M_{\text{EATLI}} \cdot \rho^k = \frac{\Delta M_{\text{EATLI}}}{1 - \rho}$$

where $\rho \approx 0.15$–$0.25$ is the second-order feedback coefficient. The total infrastructure mass saved on Mars by the EATLI landing efficiency gain is 1.18–1.33 times the first-order estimate — a compounding multiplier on an already powerful primary effect.

#### 13.3 The Civilisational Leverage Calculation

Every 1 kg of propellant produced on Mars saves 7.4 to 11.3 kg in LEO. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.08567) Combining with the ISRU mass leverage of 17.6:1 for ISRU plant mass pre-deployment, and the EATLI-induced propellant reduction of ~$\Delta m_{\text{prop,land}}$ per landing:

$$\text{Total LEO mass saving per landing} = \Delta m_{\text{prop,land}} \times 9.4 \text{ (ISRU leverage)} \times \frac{1}{1 - \rho} \text{ (feedback)}$$

For a Starship-class vehicle landing on Mars ($\Delta m_{\text{prop,land}} \approx 15 \, \text{tonnes}$ reduction in a 300-tonne vehicle):

$$\text{Total LEO saving} \approx 15{,}000 \times 9.4 \times 1.22 \approx 172 \, \text{tonnes per landing}$$

At 1,000 Mars landings over the colonisation period — a conservative estimate for a mature Mars programme — the total LEO mass saving attributable to EATLI is of the order **172,000 tonnes**. At $\$50/\text{kg}$ (Starship target to LEO), this represents $\$8.6 \, \text{billion}$ in avoided Earth-to-LEO launch costs from a single EATLI spaceport.

This is not an incremental engineering improvement. It is a structural multiplier on the economics of planetary colonisation.

