## Part VII — Stochastic Robustness

### Chapter 14 — The Fluid-Structure Interaction Problem

When a large rocket descends into a semi-enclosed electromagnetic capture tower — an environment that does not exist in any current operational system — a set of coupled fluid-structure interaction (FSI) phenomena arise that are not addressed in any prior EATLI treatment.

At low altitudes there is plume-atmospheric mixing and potential large flowfield perturbations due to plume-structure interaction. When the impinged surface is large relative to the flowfield and the flowfield is supersonic, the shock near the surface can stand off the surface several exit radii, resulting in an effective total pressure higher than that in the free plume. [Science.gov](https://www.science.gov/topicpages/p/plume+impingement+effects)

For EATLI, the descent vehicle enters a tower whose geometry creates a partially enclosed annular channel between the vehicle and the capture arm structure. As the engine exhaust plume interacts with this geometry:

**Effect 1 — Plume Recirculation**: The enclosed geometry redirects exhaust gases upward along the tower walls, creating a high-temperature, high-dynamic-pressure recirculation zone that imposes both thermal and mechanical loads on the electromagnetic coil housings.

**Effect 2 — Acoustic Loading**: Plume interaction with launch pad structures creates complex turbulent noise sources characterised by Lighthill stress tensor distributions that are considerably more complex than free jet acoustics. [NASA Technical Reports Server](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20120015223/downloads/20120015223.pdf) In the EATLI tower geometry, these acoustic loads couple into structural vibration modes of the capture arms, potentially interfering with the MR damper control system.

**Effect 3 — Sensor Obscuration**: Radar and LIDAR sensors used for precision vehicle state estimation experience multi-path reflection and signal attenuation in the plume medium, introducing measurement noise that degrades guidance accuracy precisely when it is most critical.

**Effect 4 — Electromagnetic Field Perturbation**: The hot, partially ionised exhaust plume has finite electrical conductivity, potentially perturbing the infrastructure magnetic field in the capture zone and modifying the actual braking force profile relative to the vacuum calculation.

### Chapter 15 — Proposal 4: The Stochastic Aerodynamic Wake Model

#### 15.1 Decomposition of Plume-Induced Disturbances

We decompose the total plume-induced disturbance force on the vehicle into deterministic and stochastic components:

$$F_{\text{wake}}(t) = \bar{F}_{\text{wake}}(z, v_{\text{eng}}) + \tilde{F}_{\text{wake}}(t)$$

where $\bar{F}_{\text{wake}}$ is the mean plume force (computed from Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes CFD simulation parameterised by altitude $z$ and engine throttle $v_{\text{eng}}$), and $\tilde{F}_{\text{wake}}(t)$ is the zero-mean stochastic fluctuation due to turbulent plume instabilities.

#### 15.2 The Ornstein-Uhlenbeck Plume Turbulence Model

The stochastic component $\tilde{F}_{\text{wake}}(t)$ is modelled as an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process — a mean-reverting, continuous-time stochastic process appropriate for turbulent disturbances with finite correlation time:

$$d\tilde{F}_{\text{wake}} = -\theta_F \tilde{F}_{\text{wake}} \, dt + \sigma_F \, dW_t$$

where:
- $\theta_F > 0$ is the mean-reversion rate, with $\tau_{\text{corr}} = 1/\theta_F$ the turbulence correlation time
- $\sigma_F$ is the noise intensity, calibrated from experimental plume measurements
- $W_t$ is a standard $\mathbb{R}^3$-valued Wiener process

The steady-state variance is $\text{Var}(\tilde{F}_{\text{wake}}) = \sigma_F^2 / (2\theta_F)$, and the power spectral density follows a Lorentzian profile centred at zero frequency with bandwidth $\theta_F$.

For the EATLI terminal phase, preliminary estimates based on Falcon 9 landing plume data suggest $\theta_F \approx 5 \, \text{Hz}$ (correlation time $\approx 200 \, \text{ms}$) and $\sigma_F / m \approx 0.1 \, \text{m/s}^2 \cdot \text{Hz}^{-1/2}$ — comparable to wind gust loads.

#### 15.3 Stochastic Extension of the EATLI SOCP

The deterministic vehicle dynamics are replaced with stochastic differential equations:

$$dx = f(x, u) \, dt + G_{\text{wake}}(x, t) \, dW_t$$

where $G_{\text{wake}}(x, t) = [0_3^\top, \; \sigma_F/m \cdot I_3]^\top$ is the diffusion matrix (disturbances enter through the velocity dynamics).

The stochastic minimum-propellant problem becomes:

$$\min_{u(\cdot)} \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_0^T \gamma(t) \, dt \right]$$

Subject to:
$$P\left(x(T) \notin \mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}}\right) \leq \epsilon$$

This chance-constrained problem — minimise expected propellant subject to a maximum miss probability $\epsilon$ — can be solved via two approaches:

**Approach A — Scenario-Based Stochastic Programming**: Generate $N_s$ sample paths of $W_t$, solve the deterministic SOCP for each, and select the solution that satisfies the chance constraint with confidence $1 - \delta$ using Scenario Approach theory (Campi and Garatti, 2008): $N_s \geq \lceil 2\epsilon^{-1} (\ln \delta^{-1} + n) \rceil$ where $n$ is the problem dimension. For $\epsilon = 0.01$, $\delta = 0.001$, $n = 12$: $N_s \approx 1,600$ — computationally tractable for offline trajectory planning.

**Approach B — Moment Propagation (Online)**: For real-time execution, propagate the first two moments of the state distribution (mean $\bar{x}(t)$ and covariance $\Sigma(t)$) using the linearised dynamics. The chance constraint $P(x(T) \notin \mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}}) \leq \epsilon$ becomes a deterministic constraint on $\bar{x}(T)$ and $\Sigma(T)$ via a Gaussian tail bound. This moment-propagation approach preserves the SOCP structure and adds only a linear matrix inequality (LMI) constraint to the problem, maintaining real-time solvability.

#### 15.4 Sensor Fusion for Plume-Obscured State Estimation

The plume obscuration of LIDAR/radar signals during the final approach requires a sensor fusion strategy that remains robust to measurement outages. We propose an **Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF)** formulation that:

- Incorporates the OU plume model as an augmented state variable, enabling the filter to predict plume force evolution
- Uses redundant sensor modalities (IMU, RF ranging, acoustic triangulation) that are less affected by plume obscuration
- Weights sensor inputs by a real-time signal quality metric derived from plume density estimates

The CBF Barrier 2 (velocity bound at capture zone entry) must be rewritten in terms of the estimated state $\hat{x}$ rather than the true state $x$, with a safety margin proportional to the filter uncertainty:

$$h_2^{\text{robust}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma) = v_{\text{cap,max}}^2 - \|\hat{v}\|^2 - k_{\sigma} \sqrt{\text{tr}(P_v \Sigma P_v^\top)}$$

where $P_v$ projects $\Sigma$ onto the velocity subspace and $k_{\sigma} > 0$ is a safety factor. This provides a probabilistic safety guarantee: $P(\|v\| \leq v_{\text{cap,max}}) \geq 1 - \Phi(-k_\sigma)$ where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF.

