Appendix A — Derivation of the Eddy-Current Braking Force Profile

This appendix provides the full electromagnetic derivation of the Mach/Russell bimodal force profile introduced in Chapter 5, including the underlying Maxwell equations, the skin‑effect closure, and the transition through the critical velocity.

A.1 Magnetic Diffusion and Induced Fields

Consider a conducting medium of electrical conductivity \sigma moving with velocity \mathbf{v} through a static magnetic field \mathbf{B}_0. In the magnetoquasistatic approximation (displacement current negligible), Maxwell’s equations yield the induction equation

\frac{\partial \mathbf{B}}{\partial t} = \nabla \times (\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) + \frac{1}{\mu_0 \sigma} \nabla^2 \mathbf{B}.
\tag{A.1}

For a rigid body moving at uniform velocity \mathbf{v} = v\,\hat{\mathbf{z}} through a field that is primarily transverse and steady in the laboratory frame, the convective term dominates the diffusion term at high velocity. The induced current density is

\mathbf{J} = \sigma(\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}_0) = \sigma\, v\, B_0\,\hat{\mathbf{y}},
\tag{A.2}

giving a Lorentz force density \mathbf{f} = \mathbf{J} \times \mathbf{B}_0 = -\sigma v B_0^2\,\hat{\mathbf{z}}. Integration over the conductor volume yields the linear force

F_{\text{EM}}^{\text{low}} = \sigma A_{\text{eff}} B_0^2 v,
\tag{A.3}

where A_{\text{eff}} is an effective cross‑sectional area. This linear regime holds only when the magnetic Reynolds number R_m = \mu_0 \sigma L_{\text{char}} v is small, i.e. when the induced field is negligible compared to \mathbf{B}_0.

A.2 Skin‑Effect Saturation at High Velocity

When R_m \gg 1, the induced eddy currents generate a shielding field that expels flux from the conductor interior. The penetration depth of the field (skin depth) for harmonic time dependence at angular frequency \omega \sim v/L_{\text{char}} is

\delta = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\mu_0 \sigma \omega}} = \sqrt{\frac{2 L_{\text{char}}}{\mu_0 \sigma v}}.
\tag{A.4}

The effective volume carrying current is proportional to \delta, thus the braking force saturates and eventually decays with increasing velocity. Following Mach and Russell (the classical eddy‑current brake analysis), the force‑velocity relation for a simple dipole geometry can be expressed as

F_{\text{EM}}(v) = \frac{2 F_{\max} (v/v_{\text{crit}})}{1 + (v/v_{\text{crit}})^2},
\tag{A.5}

where the critical velocity

v_{\text{crit}} = \frac{1}{\mu_0 \sigma \,\delta_{\text{ref}}}
\tag{A.6}

is the velocity at which the skin depth equals a reference length scale \delta_{\text{ref}} (typically taken as the thickness of the conductive layer or the characteristic geometric dimension), and

F_{\max} = \sigma A_{\text{eff}} B_0^2 v_{\text{crit}}
\tag{A.7}

is the maximum achievable force. Equation (A.5) interpolates smoothly between the linear low‑velocity asymptote F \propto v and the inverse‑velocity decay F \propto 1/v at high speed. The peak force occurs at v = v_{\text{crit}} and has the value F_{\max}.

A.3 Bimodal Load Profile

For a vehicle entering the active field zone at velocity v_t, the time evolution of the electromagnetic force experienced during deceleration depends on the ratio v_t / v_{\text{crit}}. Two regimes characterise the Smart Skin architecture (Section 10.1):

1. Subcritical entry (v_t \ll v_{\text{crit}}): the vehicle remains in the linear regime throughout; the force grows monotonically as velocity decreases (the deceleration causes increasing relative velocity? Actually the braking force decreases as the vehicle slows down in the linear regime; monotonic decrease from initial value).
2. Supercritical entry (v_t > v_{\text{crit}}): the vehicle enters in the saturation‑decay regime. As it slows, the force first increases as velocity approaches v_{\text{crit}} (moving down the inverse‑velocity curve), reaches a maximum at v_{\text{crit}}, and then decreases in the linear regime below v_{\text{crit}}. This produces the distinctive bimodal load profile with an early peak (at entry) and a late peak (at v_{\text{crit}}).

The precise shape of the load profile is obtained by coupling (A.5) to the vehicle dynamics \dot{v} = -(F_{\text{EM}}(v) + F_{\text{prop}})/m + g and solving the ordinary differential equation. The bimodal structure is exploited in the design of the magnetorheological damper tuning (Chapter 5.4) and in the safety barrier for capture velocity (Chapter 11.3).

A.4 Numerical Parameters for Reference

The following parameter values are used throughout the dissertation:

· Stainless steel skin: \sigma = 1.4 \times 10^6 \, \text{S/m}, \delta_{\text{ref}} = 5 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{m}, yielding v_{\text{crit}} \approx 114 \, \text{m/s}.
· Smart Skin (Cu‑Al outer layer): \sigma = 3.5 \times 10^7 \, \text{S/m}, same \delta_{\text{ref}}, giving v_{\text{crit}} \approx 4.5 \, \text{m/s}.

These values place all Earth terminal descent velocities (v_t \in [5, 30] m/s) in the linear regime for a steel skirt, but firmly in the bimodal supercritical regime for the Smart Skin. The resulting force‑time profiles are shown in Figure A.1 (not reproduced here).

