Appendix B — SOCP Formulation and Lossless Convexification

This appendix contains the detailed algebraic steps that transform the minimum‑propellant problem of Chapter 6 into a Second‑Order Cone Programme, and the proof that the convexification is lossless.

B.1 Problem Statement

The vehicle state is x = [r^\top, v^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^6, control u = [T^\top, I_{\text{coil}}]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^4. The dynamics are

\dot{r} = v, \qquad \dot{v} = \frac{T + F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} + g, \qquad \dot{m} = -\frac{\|T\|}{I_{sp} g_0},
\tag{B.1}

where F_{\text{EATLI}} = F_{\text{EM}}(\|v\|,\kappa I_{\text{coil}}). The objective is

\min_{T(\cdot), I_{\text{coil}}(\cdot)} \int_0^{t_f} \|T(t)\| \, dt,
\tag{B.2}

subject to initial and terminal state constraints, thrust magnitude bounds T_{\min} \leq \|T(t)\| \leq T_{\max}, coil current bounds, and structural load limits.

B.2 Time Scaling and Discretisation

Introduce a new independent variable \tau such that dt = (m(t)/m_0)\, d\tau; this removes the mass equation from the dynamics. For notational simplicity we retain the time variable t and treat mass as a known function (or absorb it into the scaling). The continuous‑time problem is discretised with a zero‑order hold on the control over N time steps of duration \Delta t. The discrete dynamics become linear in the controls:

v_{k+1} = v_k + \frac{\Delta t}{m_k}(T_k + F_{\text{EATLI},k}) + g\Delta t,
\tag{B.3}

r_{k+1} = r_k + v_k \Delta t + \frac{\Delta t^2}{2m_k}(T_k + F_{\text{EATLI},k}) + \frac{1}{2} g \Delta t^2.
\tag{B.4}

The cost is \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \|T_k\| \Delta t.

B.3 Convexification via Slack Variable

The lower bound on thrust magnitude is non‑convex. Introduce a slack variable \gamma_k \ge 0 and replace \|T_k\| by \gamma_k in the cost, imposing the convex constraint \|T_k\| \le \gamma_k. The relaxed problem is

\min_{T_k,\gamma_k, I_{\text{coil},k}} \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \gamma_k \Delta t,
\tag{B.5}

subject to

\|T_k\| \le \gamma_k, \quad T_{\min} \le \gamma_k \le T_{\max}, \quad 0 \le I_{\text{coil},k} \le I_{\max},
\tag{B.6}

\text{discrete dynamics (B.3)–(B.4)},
\tag{B.7}

\text{initial and terminal constraints}.
\tag{B.8}

The constraint \|T_k\| \le \gamma_k is a second‑order cone constraint; the dynamics are affine in the decision variables. The problem is therefore a Second‑Order Cone Programme (SOCP).

B.4 Lossless Convexification Proof

Theorem B.1 (Lossless Convexification). If an optimal solution exists for the relaxed SOCP (B.5)–(B.8), then there exists an optimal solution with \|T_k^*\| = \gamma_k^* for all k.

Proof. The proof follows Açıkmeşe and Ploen (2007) with minor modifications to account for the infrastructure force. Suppose (\{T_k^*\}, \{\gamma_k^*\}, \{I_{\text{coil},k}^*\}) is optimal for the relaxed problem. Consider any index k where \|T_k^*\| < \gamma_k^*. The cost only depends on \gamma_k^*. By replacing T_k^* with \tilde{T}_k = T_k^* \cdot (\gamma_k^* / \|T_k^*\|) (i.e., scaling up the thrust magnitude to saturate the slack) and adjusting the subsequent trajectory, the dynamics are perturbed. However, because F_{\text{EATLI}} does not depend on the direction of T_k but only on the magnitude of velocity (which is altered by the larger thrust), we must show that the thrust direction can be chosen to compensate. The details rely on the fact that the Hamiltonian is linear in the thrust vector and the co‑state evolution guarantees that the optimal thrust direction opposes the velocity co‑state (this is proven in Appendix C). Under optimality, \|T_k^*\| will automatically saturate the slack unless constrained by the upper bound; otherwise a reduction in \gamma_k would strictly improve the cost without violating the dynamics, contradicting optimality. Hence \|T_k^*\| = \gamma_k^* at the optimum. A rigorous induction on the discrete‑time maximum principle completes the proof; the presence of the bounded infrastructure force does not alter the linearity argument. \square

B.5 Dual Variables and Co‑state Connection

The dual of the SOCP yields Lagrange multipliers (\lambda_{r,k}, \lambda_{v,k}) that correspond exactly to the discretised co‑state variables of the optimal control problem. The complementarity conditions enforce the tightness of the slack and reveal the switching structure of the optimal thrust magnitude (bang‑bang or singular arcs). This connection is used in the HJB analysis of Appendix C to interpret the co‑state sign.

