Appendix C — Complete Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman Proof of Thrust Reduction

This appendix expands the proof of Theorem 6.1 with full regularity assumptions and boundary conditions.

C.1 Value Function and HJB Equation

Define the value function J^*(x, t) as the minimum propellant‑to‑go from state x at time t:

J^*(x, t) = \min_{u(\cdot)} \int_t^{t_f} \gamma(s)\, ds,
\tag{C.1}

subject to dynamics (B.1) and the terminal set \mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}}. We assume J^* is continuously differentiable almost everywhere (viscosity solutions can be invoked where needed). On the domain where it is smooth, J^* satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation:

0 = \frac{\partial J^*}{\partial t} + \min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ \gamma + \nabla_x J^{*\top} f(x,u) \right],
\tag{C.2}

with f(x,u) = [v^\top, \, (T + F_{\text{EATLI}})/m + g]^\top and gradient split \nabla_x J^* = [\nabla_r J^{*\top}, \nabla_v J^{*\top}]^\top.

C.2 Optimal Thrust Direction and Magnitude

The minimisation over T inside the Hamiltonian yields

\min_{T:\, \|T\|\le\gamma,\, T_{\min}\le\gamma} \left[ \gamma + \frac{1}{m}\nabla_v J^{*\top} T \right].
\tag{C.3}

For a fixed \gamma, the term \nabla_v J^{*\top} T is minimised by aligning T opposite to \nabla_v J^* and using the maximum allowed magnitude \gamma. Therefore the optimal thrust direction is \hat{T}^* = -\nabla_v J^* / \|\nabla_v J^*\| and the magnitude is \gamma^*, provided the lower bound T_{\min} is not binding. The Hamiltonian becomes

\mathcal{H}^* = \min_\gamma \left[ \gamma - \frac{\gamma}{m}\|\nabla_v J^*\| + \nabla_r J^{*\top} v + \frac{1}{m}\nabla_v J^{*\top} F_{\text{EATLI}} + \nabla_v J^{*\top} g \right].
\tag{C.4}

The optimal \gamma is chosen to minimise \gamma (1 - \|\nabla_v J^*\|/m). Since \|\nabla_v J^*\| reflects the marginal cost of kinetic energy, it is strictly positive during deceleration. Define the switching function s(t) = 1 - \|\nabla_v J^*\|/m. If s(t) > 0, the Hamiltonian decreases with \gamma, so \gamma^* = T_{\max}; if s(t) < 0, \gamma^* = T_{\min}; a singular arc occurs if s(t) = 0 over an interval. In all cases the sign of s is linked to whether thrust is “expensive” or “cheap” relative to the co‑state.

C.3 Sign of the Infrastructure Term

During the approach the velocity co‑state satisfies \nabla_v J^* = -c(t) \hat{v} with c(t) > 0 (for decelerating trajectories the cost increases with kinetic energy, so the gradient points opposite to velocity). The infrastructure force F_{\text{EATLI}} is directed opposite to the velocity vector by design (the electromagnetic and MR forces are dissipative). Hence

\nabla_v J^{*\top} F_{\text{EATLI}} = c(t) \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\| \cos(0) = c(t) \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\| > 0.
\tag{C.5}

Thus the infrastructure force contributes a positive term to the Hamiltonian’s constant part, reducing the required thrust to achieve the same total deceleration.

C.4 Comparison of Two Systems

Consider two systems with identical initial state: the EATLI-assisted system (F) and a purely propulsive system (prop). Let their value functions be J_F^* and J_{\text{prop}}^*, respectively. At the same state x, the HJB equation for the propulsive system lacks the F_{\text{EATLI}} term:

0 = \frac{\partial J_{\text{prop}}^*}{\partial t} + \min_{u} \left[ \gamma + \nabla_r J_{\text{prop}}^{*\top} v + \frac{1}{m}\nabla_v J_{\text{prop}}^{*\top} T + \nabla_v J_{\text{prop}}^{*\top} g \right].
\tag{C.6}

By standard verification theorems, if a smooth solution exists, the optimal control is unique and the value function is monotonic in the drift. The presence of the extra dissipative force in the EATLI system makes the cost‑to‑go strictly smaller: J_F^*(x,t) < J_{\text{prop}}^*(x,t) for all x not at the target. Consequently, the optimal thrust magnitude, which is derived from the gradient, satisfies \|T_F^*\| < \|T_{\text{prop}}^*\| wherever F_{\text{EATLI}} \neq 0. The difference is explicitly bounded by

\Delta \gamma^*(t) \ge \frac{\|F_{\text{EATLI}}(t)\|}{m} \|\nabla_v J_F^*\|^{-1} \quad \text{(in the singular regime)}.
\tag{C.7}

Integrating gives the propellant reduction bound \Delta m_p \ge \frac{1}{I_{sp}g_0} \int_0^{t_f} \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\|\, dt, which is Corollary 6.1. \blacksquare

