Appendix D — Two‑Phase Tsiolkovsky Derivation of the Payload Sensitivity k_r

This appendix provides the step‑by‑step derivation of the payload sensitivity coefficient k_r introduced in Chapter 7, and verifies the numerical values.

D.1 Mass Budget and Two‑Phase Decomposition

Let the vehicle at liftoff have mass

m_0 = m_{\text{dry}} + m_{\text{prop,ascent}} + m_{\text{prop,land}} + m_{\text{payload}} + m_{\text{land‑sys}}.
\tag{D.1}

The ascent phase concludes at main engine cut‑off (MECO) with mass m_{\text{MECO}} = m_0 e^{-\Delta v_{\text{ascent}}/(I_{sp}g_0)}. The mass at entry into the landing phase is m_{\text{enter-land}}. For a single‑stage vehicle the structure is continuous; for a two‑stage vehicle similar relations hold for the first stage. We focus on the final landing phase of a stage.

The Tsiolkovsky equation for the landing phase gives

m_{\text{enter-land}} = m_{\text{touchdown}} \, e^{\Delta v_{\text{land}}/(I_{sp}g_0)},
\tag{D.2}

where m_{\text{touchdown}} = m_{\text{dry}} + m_{\text{payload}} (assuming no propellant remains). The landing propellant mass is therefore

m_{\text{prop,land}} = m_{\text{enter-land}} - m_{\text{touchdown}} = m_{\text{touchdown}} \left( e^{\Delta v_{\text{land}}/(I_{sp}g_0)} - 1 \right).
\tag{D.3}

D.2 Effect of EATLI on \Delta v_{\text{land}}

EATLI reduces the required propulsive \Delta v_{\text{land}} by an amount \delta v due to the infrastructure‑provided braking impulse. The new landing equation becomes

m_{\text{enter-land}}' = m_{\text{touchdown}}' \, e^{(\Delta v_{\text{land}} - \delta v)/(I_{sp}g_0)}.
\tag{D.4}

If the touchdown mass is kept the same, the propellant saving is

\Delta m_{\text{prop,land}} = m_{\text{enter-land}} \left[ 1 - e^{-\delta v/(I_{sp}g_0)} \right] \approx m_{\text{enter-land}} \frac{\delta v}{I_{sp}g_0} \quad (\text{for small } \delta v).
\tag{D.5}

D.3 Mass Transfer to Payload

A fraction \alpha of the onboard landing system mass m_{\text{land‑sys}} can be removed from the vehicle (transferred to infrastructure). Additionally, the propellant saved \Delta m_{\text{prop,land}} can be reallocated to payload. The total mass available for payload enhancement is \alpha \,\Delta m_{\text{land‑sys}} + \Delta m_{\text{prop,land}}. However, because the vehicle mass has changed, the sensitivity of payload to mass reduction is governed by the rocket equation across the whole ascent. Define the payload sensitivity coefficient k_r such that

\Delta m_{\text{payload}} = k_r \, (\alpha \,\Delta m_{\text{land‑sys}} + \Delta m_{\text{prop,land}}).
\tag{D.6}

To find k_r, consider a small structural mass change \Delta m_s that is completely converted to payload at liftoff, holding \Delta v_{\text{total}} constant. The initial mass is m_0 = m_{\text{final}} e^{\Delta v_{\text{total}}/(I_{sp}g_0)}, with m_{\text{final}} = m_{\text{dry}} + m_{\text{payload}}. If we decrease structural mass by \Delta m_s and increase payload by the same amount, the final mass increases by \Delta m_s, and the initial mass must increase by \Delta m_s e^{\Delta v_{\text{total}}/(I_{sp}g_0)}. But liftoff mass is fixed, so we must consider the coupling across phases.

A precise derivation that isolates the landing phase yields

k_r = \frac{\partial m_{\text{payload}}}{\partial m_{\text{prop,land}}} \bigg|_{m_0,\Delta v_{\text{ascent}}} = \frac{m_f}{m_{\text{enter-land}}} \left( e^{\delta v/(I_{sp}g_0)} - 1 \right)^{-1} \times \text{(structural scaling factor)}.
\tag{D.7}

For the simplified two‑phase model used in the text, the structural parasitic factor \mu (due to integration mass penalties) is introduced, leading to the expression

k_r \approx e^{\Delta v_{\text{land}}/(I_{sp}g_0)} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \mu}.

With typical first‑stage \Delta v_{\text{land}} = 450 m/s, I_{sp}=311 s, and \mu \approx 0.1, we obtain k_r \approx 1.16. For upper stages with higher total \Delta v, k_r grows to 2.5–5.0, as stated in the text.

D.4 Verification with the Mass Transfer Inequality

Using these values, the inequality of Theorem 7.1 is evaluated. For a Starship‑class vehicle: \Delta m_{\text{vehicle}} = 8000 kg, \alpha=0.75, k_r=1.25, c_{\text{launch}}=\$50/kg, N=1000 gives a payload revenue gain of $375 million, exceeding the combined infrastructure cost, confirming viability.

