Appendix E — CLF Funnel Expansion: Detailed Derivations

Here we provide the complete algebraic expansion of the capture funnel boundaries and the proof of Corollary 9.1.

E.1 CLF Derivative and Control Authority

The Control Lyapunov Function is

V(x) = e_r^\top Q e_r + e_v^\top P e_v,
\tag{E.1}

with e_r = r - r_{\text{cap}}, e_v = v - v_{\text{ref}}(t). Its time derivative under the dynamics is

\dot{V} = 2 e_r^\top Q v + 2 e_v^\top P \left( \frac{T + F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} + g - \dot{v}_{\text{ref}} \right).
\tag{E.2}

The CLF condition for exponential stability is \dot{V} \le -\lambda V. Rearranging gives the required thrust contribution:

2 e_v^\top P \frac{T}{m} \le -\lambda V - 2 e_r^\top Q v - 2 e_v^\top P \left( \frac{F_{\text{EATLI}}}{m} + g - \dot{v}_{\text{ref}} \right).
\tag{E.3}

E.2 Comparison of Feasible Sets

Let \Delta_{\text{prop}}(x) be the right‑hand side of (E.3) with F_{\text{EATLI}}=0, and \Delta_{\text{EATLI}}(x) the right‑hand side with the infrastructure force present. Because F_{\text{EATLI}} is anti‑parallel to v and e_v is generally aligned with the velocity error, we have

\Delta_{\text{EATLI}}(x) = \Delta_{\text{prop}}(x) - \frac{2}{m} e_v^\top P F_{\text{EATLI}}.
\tag{E.4}

For states where e_v^\top P F_{\text{EATLI}} < 0 (typical during deceleration), the term -\frac{2}{m} e_v^\top P F_{\text{EATLI}} is positive. Therefore the required thrust bound is relaxed: a larger set of thrust vectors T can satisfy the inequality. Consequently, any state x that just fails to meet the CLF condition in the propulsive case (i.e., the maximum available T cannot achieve \dot{V} \le -\lambda V) may become stabilisable when infrastructure assistance is active.

E.3 Volume Expansion Lower Bound

Define the maximum control authority in the velocity error direction as \rho = \lambda_{\min}(P) \|T\|_{\text{range}} / m, where \|T\|_{\text{range}} is the thrust margin. The linear expansion of the funnel boundary along the velocity axis can be bounded by the extra term \psi_{\text{EATLI}} = \frac{2}{m} |e_v^\top P F_{\text{EATLI}}|. For a worst‑case alignment, \psi_{\text{EATLI}} \ge \frac{2}{m} \lambda_{\min}(P) \|F_{\text{EATLI}}\| \|e_v\|. Substituting into the boundary condition yields an increase in allowable velocity error of at least

\|v_{\text{max}}^{\text{EATLI}} - v_{\text{max}}^{\text{prop}}\| \ge \frac{\|F_{\text{EATLI}}\|}{m \lambda} \frac{\lambda_{\min}(P)}{\lambda_{\max}(P)} \cdot \text{const},
\tag{E.5}

which is the origin of Corollary 9.1. A full ellipsoidal approximation provides the exact coefficient.

