Appendix F — CBF‑CLF Quadratic Programme: KKT Conditions and Real‑Time Solvability

This appendix details the optimisation problem from Chapter 11 and proves that a solution can be obtained analytically in bounded time.

F.1 QP Formulation

At each time step, given nominal control u_{\text{nom}} = T_{\text{nom}}, the QP is

\min_{u,\delta} \frac{1}{2} \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|_H^2 + p\,\delta^2
\tag{F.1}

subject to

A_{\text{CLF}} u \le b_{\text{CLF}} + \delta,
\tag{F.2}

A_{\text{CBF},i} u \ge b_{\text{CBF},i}, \quad i=1,2,3,
\tag{F.3}

u_{\min} \le u \le u_{\max}.
\tag{F.4}

Here A_{\text{CLF}} = \frac{2}{m} e_v^\top P (a row vector), and the right‑hand side is derived from (E.3). The CBF constraints are linear in u because the barriers h_i are chosen such that \dot{h}_i = L_f h_i + L_g h_i \, u with L_g h_i being a constant matrix.

F.2 KKT Conditions

The Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{2}\|u-u_{\text{nom}}\|_H^2 + p\delta^2 + \lambda_{\text{CLF}}(A_{\text{CLF}} u - b_{\text{CLF}} - \delta) - \sum_i \lambda_i (A_{\text{CBF},i} u - b_{\text{CBF},i}) - \nu^\top (u - u_{\min}) + \mu^\top (u - u_{\max}).
\tag{F.5}

Stationarity yields

u = u_{\text{nom}} - H^{-1}\left( A_{\text{CLF}}^\top \lambda_{\text{CLF}} - \sum_i A_{\text{CBF},i}^\top \lambda_i - \nu + \mu \right),
\tag{F.6}

2p\delta - \lambda_{\text{CLF}} = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \delta = \lambda_{\text{CLF}}/(2p).
\tag{F.7}

Complementarity conditions: \lambda_{\text{CLF}} \ge 0, \lambda_{\text{CLF}}(A_{\text{CLF}} u - b_{\text{CLF}} - \delta) = 0; \lambda_i \ge 0, \lambda_i (A_{\text{CBF},i} u - b_{\text{CBF},i}) = 0; analogous for bounds.

Because the problem is strictly convex, the solution is unique and can be found by checking the finite set of active constraint combinations. The QP has at most 4 inequality constraints and box constraints, making analytical solution via active‑set enumeration possible in O(2^4) operations, well within a 100 μs budget on flight‑qualified processors.

F.3 Safety Guarantee

If a feasible solution exists at time t and the CBF constraints are satisfied with strict positivity, the forward invariance of the safe set is guaranteed by the CBF condition \dot{h}_i + \alpha(h_i) \ge 0 for the chosen control. In the stochastic case (Appendix G), the barriers are robustified to account for estimation uncertainty.

