Appendix G — Stochastic Plume Model and Chance‑Constrained SOCP

This appendix develops the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck plume turbulence model, derives the moment propagation equations, and outlines the scenario approach for chance constraints.

G.1 OU Process Model

The stochastic component of the plume‑induced force is modelled as a vector Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process:

d\tilde{F}_{\text{wake}} = -\theta \tilde{F}_{\text{wake}} dt + \Sigma \, dW_t,
\tag{G.1}

with \theta > 0 and \Sigma = \sigma I_3. The steady‑state variance is \sigma^2/(2\theta). The correlation time is \tau = 1/\theta. Based on Falcon 9 landing data, \theta = 5 Hz, \sigma = 0.1\, m/s^2\cdot\text{Hz}^{-1/2} per unit mass.

G.2 Linearised Dynamics and Moment Propagation

The vehicle SDE is

dx = (Ax + Bu + c) dt + G dW_t,
\tag{G.2}

where x includes position, velocity, and plume states. For real‑time guidance we linearise about a reference trajectory. The mean \bar{x} and covariance \Sigma_x evolve as

\dot{\bar{x}} = A\bar{x} + Bu + c,
\tag{G.3}

\dot{\Sigma}_x = A\Sigma_x + \Sigma_x A^\top + G\Sigma G^\top.
\tag{G.4}

G.3 Chance‑Constrained SOCP Extension

The terminal capture constraint r(T) \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}} is replaced by P(r(T) \notin \mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}}) \le \epsilon. Assuming Gaussian disturbances, this is equivalent to

\mathcal{T}_{\text{cap}} \text{ contains the } (1-\epsilon)\text{‑confidence ellipsoid of } \bar{r}(T).
\tag{G.5}

For a rectangular capture zone, this becomes a set of linear constraints on \bar{r}(T) and an additional second‑order cone constraint involving the covariance (typically a linear matrix inequality approximable as SOC). The resulting problem remains convex and solvable in real time. The scenario approach sample size is N_s \ge \frac{2}{\epsilon}(\ln\frac{1}{\delta} + n) as stated.

G.4 Robust CBF for Uncertain State

When only an estimate \hat{x} with covariance \Sigma_x is available, the safe set boundary condition is tightened. For Barrier 2 (velocity bound), we use

h_2^{\text{robust}}(\hat{x}) = v_{\text{cap,max}}^2 - \|\hat{v}\|^2 - k_\sigma \sqrt{\text{tr}(P_v \Sigma_x P_v^\top)} \ge 0,
\tag{G.6}

where k_\sigma = \sqrt{2}\,\text{erf}^{-1}(2p_{\text{safe}}-1) for a desired safety probability p_{\text{safe}}. This ensures that even with estimation errors the true state remains within the safe set with high probability.

