Appendix H — Mars ISRU Feedback Loop Derivation

This appendix details the geometric series model for the compounding mass efficiency gain and verifies the numerical multiplier.

H.1 First‑Order and Feedback Effects

Let \Delta M_{\text{EATLI}} be the direct reduction in Earth‑launched mass enabled by EATLI on a Mars mission (propellant saving multiplied by ISRU leverage). Part of this saving reduces the required ISRU plant size, which itself reduces the power and infrastructure mass that must be landed, thereby reducing the landing propellant further. Define the feedback coefficient \rho as the fraction of the saved mass that propagates into further landing propellant reduction in the next cycle. The total cumulative saving is the sum of a geometric series:

\Delta M_{\text{total}} = \Delta M_{\text{EATLI}} (1 + \rho + \rho^2 + \cdots) = \frac{\Delta M_{\text{EATLI}}}{1 - \rho}.
\tag{H.1}

H.2 Estimation of \rho

Let \beta be the fractional reduction in ISRU plant mass per unit reduction in propellant requirement (typically \beta \approx 0.1–0.2 kg plant/kg propellant). The ISRU plant mass reduction \Delta m_{\text{ISRU}} leads to a reduction in landed payload mass, which in turn requires less landing propellant. The cycle gain is

\rho = \frac{\Delta m_{\text{prop,land}}'}{\Delta M_{\text{EATLI}}} \approx \beta \cdot \frac{\partial m_{\text{prop,land}}}{\partial m_{\text{landed}}}.
\tag{H.2}

With typical Mars landing mass sensitivities, \rho \approx 0.15\text{–}0.25. The total multiplier 1/(1-\rho) is then 1.18–1.33, as quoted.

H.3 Earth‑to‑LEO Mass Saving

Combining the ISRU mass leverage ratio L_{\text{ISRU}} = 9.4 (average of 7.4–11.3) and the EATLI feedback multiplier, the total LEO mass saving per kilogram of EATLI‑induced propellant reduction on Mars is L_{\text{ISRU}}/(1-\rho) ≈ 11.1–12.5. For \Delta m_{\text{prop,land}} = 15 tonnes per landing, this gives ~167–188 tonnes LEO saving per landing, consistent with the value 172 tonnes used in the text.

